headers {
  X-API-Key: {{apiKey}}
  X-User-Token: {{userToken}}
}

docs {
  # archive-api

  Requests run top to bottom as a flow: each one stores the ids the next needs
  as runtime variables, so nothing has to be pasted between them. Use the
  collection runner, or run folders in order.

  ## Before the first run

  Pick the **local** environment, then start the API:

  ```
  make dev-up && make dev-setup && make run
  ```

  `apiKey` matches `API_KEY` in your `.env` (`dev-key` by default).

  `userToken` is a Cognito **access** token, and it is the one thing you have
  to fetch yourself, because this service never issues tokens - it only
  verifies them. The frontend talks to Cognito; this API never does.

  ```
  aws cognito-idp admin-initiate-auth \
    --user-pool-id $COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID \
    --client-id $COGNITO_APP_CLIENT_ID \
    --auth-flow ADMIN_USER_PASSWORD_AUTH \
    --auth-parameters USERNAME=test@example.com,PASSWORD='Temp1234!'
  ```

  Copy `AuthenticationResult.AccessToken` into the environment's `userToken`.
  It is a secret variable, so it stays out of the committed files. Tokens last
  12 hours; a sudden run of 401s usually means yours expired.

  An **ID** token will be rejected - the verifier requires `token_use` to be
  `access`.

  ## The production environment

  Production sits behind a Lambda Function URL with `AWS_IAM` auth, so every
  request also needs a SigV4 signature. The environment sets Bruno's own AWS
  auth for that; fill in credentials for the IAM user allowed to invoke the
  function. Without them you get a bare `Forbidden` from AWS before any of
  this API's own code runs.
}
